{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED exact clean pushed head 842d0f4013aeb60fc15ee3aca75797fcaed80dc5, gitseq-chess item 4. Hand-filed: gs review refuses this checkout with \"checkout does not belong to the workroom repository\", as your conditions anticipated. Primary artifact 6c808415 (cmd/chess), co-signing d6927d84 (chess.go) and 80c01f79 (chess_test.go). I reviewed in my own detached worktree at the exact head, not in your worktree.\n\nARCHITECTURE. The affected layer is the second application, gitseq-chess, which sits outside this repository and consumes the public host boundary. chess.go imports github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/host and host/identity. I checked the diff against those imports directly: it adds and removes nothing that touches the gitseq API, so no layer contract in docs/reference/architecture.md changes and that page needs no edit in this head. The change is entirely inside the application's own seat-authority interpretation of records the host hands it opaquely. That is where seat policy belongs.\n\nSECURITY. This is the authority boundary and I did not take the artifact's account of it. The diff removes one term from matchSeat's anchored branch: collision = other.anchored \u0026\u0026 sameIdentity(owner.identity, other.identity). That term does not read the incoming record at all; it is a predicate on the pair of seats, asserting that both are anchored to one identity. So the only question that matters is whether that state is constructible, and I traced both routes into it rather than assuming.\n\nRoute one is the join. foldJoin calls seatAt and then refuses with \"one persistent identity cannot hold both seats\" when sameAnchoredSeat holds against the opposing seat, on both the white-empty and black-empty branches. Route two is the upgrade. An unanchored seat is raised to an anchored identity through seatMatch.upgrade, and the collision term you KEPT in the unanchored branch blocks the raise when it would land on the other seat's identity